            <ArticleTitle>Decreased intestinal acetylcholinesterase in early Parkinson disease: An 11C-donepezil PET study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>775-781</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1212/WNL.0000000000003633</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate systemic levels of acetylcholinesterase in early Parkinson disease (PD) with 11C-donepezil PET, a potential marker of parasympathetic innervation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This was a cross-sectional study with 19 patients with early-stage PD (disease duration 1.5 ± 0.6 years) and 16 age-matched controls who had clinical assessments, olfaction tests, and 11C-donepezil PET to measure acetylcholinesterase density in peripheral organs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The patients with PD showed significantly reduced 11C-donepezil uptake in the small intestine (-14%, p = 0.018), colon (-22%, p &lt; 0.001), and kidneys (-14%, p = 0.028). No difference in myocardial or pancreatic acetylcholinesterase levels was seen.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We found significantly decreased 11C-donepezil signal in the intestine and kidneys of patients with early PD, suggesting that parasympathetic denervation is present early in the disease course.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 American Academy of Neurology.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
